# Line endings.
#
# The repository stores LF. What lands in a working tree is up to that
# checkout's core.autocrlf, which on Windows means CRLF - harmless for a file
# PHP reads, and not harmless for one the shell runs. A script whose first line
# is "#!/usr/bin/env php" followed by a CR sends env looking for a program
# called "php\r", and the error names a file that looks perfectly fine in an
# editor. Both of these are run as programs, and deploy/build.php copies the
# working tree into the bundle, so a CRLF checkout here is a CRLF file on the
# server. They keep Unix endings wherever they are checked out.
backup-database.php text eol=lf
spark               text eol=lf
